WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.080
Jigsaw’s infamous bathroom game didn’t
play out quite like he drew it up in his plans.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.950
To hear about the key detail that could have
changed everything, stick around to the end

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:08.950
of this video.

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:11.520
This video is sponsored by The CZsWorld Merch
Store.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:18.420
Welcome to another episode of Things You Missed.

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:20.449
This week, I want to play a game.

00:00:20.449 --> 00:00:23.300
So we’ll be covering the first major motion
picture from James Wan.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.730
I feel like I’ve talked a lot about Wan’s
work a lot on the channel, and I have talked

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:30.050
about his franchises before, but this is actually
the first time I’ve done a Things You Missed

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.010
specifically on a film he directed.

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:34.760
So the clock is ticking, let’s let the game
begin.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:42.130
The first item of note comes in before the
movie even starts, when we see the Twisted

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:43.579
Pictures logo animation.

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:47.059
The company was started in order to produce
Saw, and the intro is inspired by the Razor

00:00:47.059 --> 00:00:50.469
Wire Trap, which is the second trap in the
movie, where a man named Paul is asked to

00:00:50.469 --> 00:00:54.079
climb out of a cage riddled with barbed wire
and cut himself in order to escape.

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:57.610
The movie itself opens by showing us a little
light floating around the bathtub.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:01.180
We don’t know what it is yet, but if you
look very closely it’s attached to the key

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:05.100
to Adam’s shackle, which goes down the drain
that opens when he wakes up in a panic.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.340
In Saw III, we find out that it was Amanda
who placed the key there, and I think she

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:11.250
was supposed to put it somewhere else to give
Adam a chance to get it.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:14.869
This means the game was designed with the
possibility of Adam being able to walk freely

00:01:14.869 --> 00:01:17.770
around the bathroom, and there is more evidence
of this later on.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:21.200
Dr. Gordon is already awake at the beginning
of the movie, and he demonstrates the reason

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.889
that Jigsaw has tested him, his lack of passion
for life, in his opening dialogue.

00:01:24.889 --> 00:01:26.189
“You’re not dead.”

00:01:26.189 --> 00:01:27.189
“Who’s that?”

00:01:27.189 --> 00:01:29.229
“There’s no point in yelling.

00:01:29.229 --> 00:01:30.539
I already tried...”

00:01:30.539 --> 00:01:31.849
He sounds defeated.

00:01:31.849 --> 00:01:34.030
He sounds like he doesn’t care if he lives
or dies.

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:37.649
The goal of Jigsaw’s game was basically
to change that, to enlighten these prisoners

00:01:37.649 --> 00:01:38.649
on the joys of life.

00:01:38.649 --> 00:01:42.829
To symbolize this enlightenment, we see a
big booming shot of the overhead lights literally

00:01:42.829 --> 00:01:44.369
turning on above them.

00:01:44.369 --> 00:01:47.409
This would also allow the glow-in-the-dark
paint to begin to charge.

00:01:47.409 --> 00:01:49.419
The bathroom itself is very strange.

00:01:49.419 --> 00:01:51.959
It’s a dilapidated men’s room, but with
no dividers.

00:01:51.959 --> 00:01:56.389
It looks commercial; there are multiple toilets,
sinks and showers, so the inclusion of a bathtub

00:01:56.389 --> 00:01:57.389
is strange.

00:01:57.389 --> 00:02:00.429
I think the bathtub represents Jigsaw’s
idea of rebirth.

00:02:00.429 --> 00:02:04.070
Adam awakes from it and is thrust into the
cold reality of the bathroom, just as we are

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:06.930
born from the womb into the harsh actuality
of our world.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:09.860
We later learn that the bathroom is located
in a tunnel network underground.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:13.950
John was once an engineer for low-income living
solutions, so I wonder if the bathroom was

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.140
once a part of some kind of communal shelter.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:20.079
However, the sign seen in the tunnels at the
end of the movie says CAUTION: Raw Sewage.

00:02:20.079 --> 00:02:23.920
So perhaps the bathroom was connected to some
kind of sewage treatment center.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:28.319
When Adam first noticed John hiding in plain
sight, PRETENDING to be dead, he ironically

00:02:28.319 --> 00:02:29.319
makes an observation.

00:02:29.319 --> 00:02:31.810
“First dead body I’ve ever seen.

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:33.480
Different in real life.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:34.739
They don’t move.”

00:02:34.739 --> 00:02:39.069
...which is a nice touch considering what
happens in the final scene and really adds

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:40.329
to Adam’s sense of terror.

00:02:40.329 --> 00:02:43.760
After beginning to solve the clues of the
game, Adam obtains the tape recorder and puts

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:44.900
in his tape.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:55.040
“If anyone ever uses the excuse it’s just
a game, I’m gonna lose my mind.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:56.620
I’m gonna lose it, like are you kidding
me?

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:57.440
You’re so stupid.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:02.800
If anyone that ever uses the excuse that it’s
just a game, is a horrible human being.

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:03.940
And is lazy.”

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:05.320
“I don’t get it.”

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:09.380
There is a little clue left on Dr. Gordon’s
tape about the identity of Jigsaw.

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:10.560
Let’s roll that clip.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.240
“...thing left to do is shoot yourself.”

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:20.440
*coughing*

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:22.820
(This is why we have to have social distancing.)

00:03:22.829 --> 00:03:26.152
The coughing alludes to the fact that Gordon
already knows the killer, or he would if he

00:03:26.152 --> 00:03:29.760
had taken more time to appreciate the human
side of his dying patients.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.120
“His name is John,

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:33.280
Dr. Gordon.

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:36.240
He’s a very interesting person.”

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:39.219
The other clue whispered at the end of the
tape, “follow your heart”, leads Adam

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:40.829
to find the hacksaws.

00:03:40.829 --> 00:03:43.480
As the audience, we can see that there is
something else in the bag (the photos Adam

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:46.510
had taken of Dr. Gordon) but Gordon himself
cannot see them.

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:50.719
Gordon eventually realizes that he and Adam
may have been placed in the bathroom by Jigsaw,

00:03:50.719 --> 00:03:53.849
the unknown serial killer that Dr. Gordon
was once a suspect for.

00:03:53.849 --> 00:03:57.450
The cops who questioned him are Stephen Sing
and David Tapp, and we see a flashback of

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:59.550
their first encounters at Jigsaw crime scenes.

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:02.360
They are introduced to the remains of Paul
by Alison Kerry.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:05.540
One thing I found interesting is that her
badge says she’s the Deputy Coroner.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:09.389
In Saw 2, she’s a Homicide Detective for
the newly formed serial killer task force,

00:04:09.389 --> 00:04:13.349
so that tells me that the loss of Detectives
Sing and Tapp resulted in her getting a promotion.

00:04:13.349 --> 00:04:16.800
There is also some important information on
the evidence bag telling us the date that

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.450
Paul was found was March 19th 2004.

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:22.680
This is shortly before a 6 month timeskip,
which lands the date of the bathroom game

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:26.410
somewhere in October of 2004, which is also
when Saw was released.

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:30.590
We’re first introduced to who John Kramer
is in Gordon’s flashback at the hospital,

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:35.030
and John has openly displayed his design sketch
for the reverse bear trap on the table.

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:37.050
Just like for anyone to see while he’s asleep
I guess.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:40.770
If you’re gonna become a serial killer,
maybe don’t do that.

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:41.770
Just a suggestion.

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:44.560
But if you do, you might want to hide the
evidence just a little bit.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:48.840
There’s a green X on the opposite page perhaps
symbolic of the X marks the spot from the

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:49.840
bathroom game.

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:52.930
Gordon ends up being cleared as a suspect,
but sticks around to hear the testimony of

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:56.620
the first survivor, Amanda, who tells the
story of how she woke up in a room and a tape

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:57.620
started playing.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:04:58.840
“Hello Amanda.”

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:03.330
The upcoming ninth Saw movie is called Spiral,
and the name draws inspiration from the spirals

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:07.110
on the cheeks of Billy the Puppet and later,
the large spiral seen in John’s workshop

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:08.310
at the mannequin factory.

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:12.060
The spiraling camera movement when John is
first shown on screen, you know, the one where

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.050
he’s PRETENDING to be dead, could also be
a connection.

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:18.240
In my Spiral trailer analysis I talked about
how I think the Spiral could be kind of like

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.770
a new religious icon that John is inventing,
but I guess we’ll find out when the movie

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:22.770
comes out.

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:26.199
When Amanda goes to try to retrieve the key
to reverse bear trap, there’s a secret message

00:05:26.199 --> 00:05:27.889
from Jigsaw hidden on the wall.

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:32.699
If you pause at just the right moment, you
can read it.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:43.139
The message on the wall in Amanda’s cell
reads:

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:44.220
If you try to
keep your life for

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:44.880
yourself.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:45.580
You will
lose it.

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:47.240
But if you can give
your life to me you will

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:48.580
find true life.

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:52.660
This message doesn’t seem to apply to Amanda,
but it may have been a part of a previous

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:55.910
game for the man described as being her cellmate,
Donnie Greco.

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:59.240
Donnie doesn’t seem to have much of a chance
to survive, he is essentially neutralized

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:02.180
while Amanda is forced to cut through his
stomach in order to retrieve her key.

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:05.419
But Jigsaw always believes in giving his victims
a second chance.

00:06:05.419 --> 00:06:09.780
So I think Donnie already lost his game, and
his punishment was that he would now die in

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:10.780
Amanda’s game.

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:13.550
This message was probably part of the rules
for Donnie’s first game.

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:17.020
When Amanda cuts open his stomach, the camera
cuts to a shadow on the wall, a reference

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:21.250
to a technique seen in early German expressionism
films, like one of the earliest influential

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:23.320
horror movies ever, Nosferatu.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:32.720
I should also note that many of the stylish
techniques used in Nosferatu were done to

00:06:32.729 --> 00:06:36.530
save money, as Germany was still picking up
the pieces from World War 1 at the time it

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:37.530
was made.

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:40.789
Saw was also made for a very low budget, but
rather than picking up the pieces from World

00:06:40.789 --> 00:06:44.400
War 1, the producers of Saw were picking up
the pieces of a second mortgage that they

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:47.750
had to put on their production headquarters,
and luckily for them, Saw ended up being very

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:50.139
successful and spawning many sequels.

00:06:50.139 --> 00:06:53.110
While those sequels may not have been planned
from the beginning, I’d still like to point

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:56.090
out something I found interesting about Lawrence
Gordon’s home.

00:06:56.090 --> 00:06:59.289
This will contain spoilers for the series,
so skip ahead 20 seconds if you haven’t

00:06:59.289 --> 00:07:01.960
seen the rest of the movies.

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:05.400
Gordon has this clock in his living room,
with these little ornaments that look vaguely

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:07.010
like Jigsaw puzzle pieces.

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:10.060
This gear could also represent the idea of
mechanical engineering.

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:12.280
As you may know, Jigsaw was once a civil engineer.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:15.199
And I like to think that these could be little
clues that Lawrence Gordon would one day make

00:07:15.199 --> 00:07:17.180
the perfect successor to Jigsaw.

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:20.840
There are clocks all over Gordon’s house,
including both an hourglass and analog clock

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:25.169
on his desk, and that all ties into part of
the reason behind Jigsaw’s work: time.

00:07:25.169 --> 00:07:28.770
All of this starts when John finds out from
Dr. Gordon how much time he has to live.

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:31.710
When he learns that, he learns how valuable
his time on this earth really is.

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:35.430
Back in the present, at the bathroom, Dr.
Gordon tells Adam about his favorite picture

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:38.520
of his family, which all three of them are
in, rare because someone usually has to take

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:39.520
the photo.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:42.270
The shot is missing from his wallet, but I
think we actually do see it in his living

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:44.370
room if you pause during the chase at the
end of the movie.

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:48.030
I mean, it makes sense considering that this
family seems to just keep reusing the same

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:50.039
few photos all over the place.

00:07:50.039 --> 00:07:51.949
Over and over.

00:07:51.949 --> 00:07:53.060
And OVER!

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:56.169
The next flashback shows Gordon putting his
daughter to bed, and he tries to calm her

00:07:56.169 --> 00:07:59.139
fears by doing the “This Little Piggy”
nursery rhyme with her.

00:07:59.139 --> 00:08:03.139
In John Kramer’s MO, pigs represent rebirth,
that’s why all of his followers don the

00:08:03.139 --> 00:08:05.199
pig masks when carrying out tasks for Jigsaw.

00:08:05.199 --> 00:08:09.080
After he leaves, his daughter sees Zep hiding
in the closet, in what almost certainly seems

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:11.789
to be an homage to that scene from Black Christmas.

00:08:11.789 --> 00:08:13.409
Roll it.

00:08:13.409 --> 00:08:14.930
“Agnes…

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:15.580
Agnes…”

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:16.640
“Huh?”

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:22.080
Meanwhile, Detective Tapp has been unable
to accept Lawrence Gordon’s innocence even

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:26.280
after the doctor provides an alibi, and spies
on him from a messy apartment across the way.

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:29.879
What I cannot answer is why Detective Tapp
has Amy’s coffee though.

00:08:29.879 --> 00:08:31.469
What have you done to Amy, Detective Tapp?

00:08:31.469 --> 00:08:33.860
“What did you do to them you bastard?”

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:37.460
Yeah, the apartment is a mess, and the walls
are covered in reconnaissance photos of the

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:40.630
doctor and newspaper headlines about the Jigsaw
murders.

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:44.120
Surprisingly, none of the names or authors
mentioned in these articles are easter eggs

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:45.590
or references to anything.

00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:49.050
There is one connection that would be made
retro-actively, when Saw: The Video Game was

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:50.450
released in 2009.

00:08:50.450 --> 00:08:55.230
The author of this article is Oswald McGillicutty,
a name that was given to the journalist character

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:58.110
you save in the game, although the game is
not considered canon.

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:01.630
The actual contents of the article, as we
often see in movies, have nothing to do with

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:02.630
the headline.

00:09:02.630 --> 00:09:05.920
This one is about a toy called Goofy Clay
that exploded and destroyed a trailer home

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:06.920
in Reno.

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:10.290
The only other semi-interesting tidbit I found
in these headlines was this article, which

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:14.470
references Stanley Kubrick’s 2001 A Space
Odyssey, which doesn’t really have anything

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:17.500
to do with Saw, but is just a well respected
movie in general.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:20.910
There would be a more meaningful reference
shortly after, when Sing and Tapp discover

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:22.920
the location of Jigsaw’s hideout.

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:26.420
The map they use doesn’t seem to correlate
to any real world locations that I could find

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:30.080
on Google Maps, but they end up determining
that the fire alarm heard in the tape must

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:33.490
have been from an emergency at 213 Stygian
Street.

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:37.430
Stygian Street is a reference to James Wan’s
first independent film, Stygian, and the address

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:42.650
number 213 is a reference to the area code
of Lacy Production Center, where Saw was filmed.

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:46.470
When they arrived there the workshop contained
a miniature version of The Bathroom, but Dr.

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:50.600
Gordon’s shackle is replaced with an electrical
wire which is connected to this battery.

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:54.220
This is perhaps our first clue about the electric
shock component connected to each of their

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:55.220
chains.

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:57.660
Jigsaw first used this on Adam, to ensure
he wasn’t cheating,

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:01.700
then on Gordon

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:03.940
to try to snap him out of his miserable wallowing,

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:08.500
then again on Adam to make him drop

00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:09.800
the gun he was holding,

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:15.440
and then on the
audience, because the ending was a shocking

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:16.400
twist...

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:19.980
yeah, ok I’ll go off myself for
that pun.

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:25.020
John’s workshop also provided another clue
about the true method that Adam could have

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:27.500
used to win and survive the bathroom game.

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:41.760
The reason that the miniature version of the
bathroom contains figures to represent Gordon

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:45.970
and Jigsaw but not Adam could be because when
we see this, it’s still 6 months before

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:50.160
the game takes place, and Adam had not yet
been hired to photograph Gordon, and so, was

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:51.970
not yet a target of John Kramer.

00:10:51.970 --> 00:10:56.080
However, it’s clear that the game only works
with a second player, so I think the fact

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:59.380
that we don’t see Adam in this diagram,
is because of what I mentioned near the beginning

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:00.390
of this video.

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:04.190
Adam was supposed to have an opportunity to
retrieve his key and have the ability to free

00:11:04.190 --> 00:11:06.190
roam around the bathroom and possibly escape.

00:11:06.190 --> 00:11:10.110
I’m not saying he definitely was supposed
to get out of his shackle, otherwise why bother

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:13.480
with the whole electric shock component, but
it seems like freedom to move about may have

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:15.270
been something Adam could have earned.

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:18.270
Also, this may have been the same miniature
used by the set designers of the movie when

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:22.440
they created the actual bathroom, because
it actually says Saw Bathroom Set right here.

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:25.180
Guess we can call this James Wan’s coffee
cup moment.

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:28.200
Zep watches the game unfold from a little
area setup within Gordon’s home.

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:31.510
I noticed there is a microphone there, which
made me wonder if there was ever supposed

00:11:31.510 --> 00:11:34.410
to be an aspect of the game where Zep communicates
with them directly.

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:38.790
Or maybe Zep was just planning on starting
a little podcast, “The Jigsaw Victim Podcast”.

00:11:38.790 --> 00:11:41.150
Let’s see you ****ing top that Bobby Dagen!

00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:44.940
Zep watches as Gordon discovers the hidden
box containing the phone causing him to remember

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:47.560
night he was captured, and every time I watch
it it makes me ****ing crazy.

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:53.940
I mean, why would Adam, who is trying to SECRETLY
spy on Dr. Gordon use the flash while taking

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:54.860
photos of him?

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:55.820
I mean, that makes no sense.

00:11:55.820 --> 00:11:57.580
That’s just the dumbest thing I’ve ever
seen.

00:11:57.590 --> 00:11:58.590
I’m sorry, I’m sorry.

00:11:58.590 --> 00:11:59.930
But you’re an idiot Adam.

00:11:59.930 --> 00:12:00.930
You’re an idiot.

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:02.020
“You know who I am.”

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:06.020
Lawrence becomes more suspicious of Adam’s
secrets, and rightfully so, and asks what

00:12:06.020 --> 00:12:07.280
else he may be hiding.

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:10.100
“Well let’s see.

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:16.170
On my sixth birthday, my best friend at the
time, Scott Tibbs, stabbed me with a rusty

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:17.300
nail, I didn’t tell you about that.”

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:21.800
On the DVD for Saw II, there is a bonus video
called The Scott Tibbs Documentary which is

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:23.750
a found footage short created by Scott.

00:12:23.750 --> 00:12:26.040
“I had to find out more!

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:32.000
So I acquired this sweet little camera, and
I’m making my own documentary about this

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:32.800
sick ****er!

00:12:33.770 --> 00:12:35.640
Scott Tibbs style baby!”

00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:38.260
Yeah, it’s not very good.

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:43.020
OK, it’s kind of one of the most embarrassing
things I’ve seen a professional film studio

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:46.660
put out, and it’s considered to be non-canon
to the Saw story, but I did want to bring

00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:50.760
it up because the character Scott himself
is still canon, and there will be more references

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:53.170
to him in future Things You Missed episodes.

00:12:53.170 --> 00:12:56.830
When it comes down to the end of the game,
Lawrence finally caves in, saws off his foot,

00:12:56.830 --> 00:13:00.560
gets the bullet and the gun... but decides
to try the play dead trick with Adam one last

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:01.140
time.

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:05.380
This attempt was a little bit smarter because
it didn’t require any… acting ability

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:06.610
on Adam’s part.

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:08.700
We all know how that turned out last time.

00:13:22.660 --> 00:13:25.380
I think Lawrence gives the signal when the
camera cuts to the back of his head right

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:27.320
here, where he probably winks at Adam.

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:31.230
The play dead trick also calls back to earlier
in the film, where Jigsaw does the same thing

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:32.230
to trick Sing.

00:13:32.230 --> 00:13:35.250
It ends up being used many times throughout
the Saw franchise, so I’ll be sure to point

00:13:35.250 --> 00:13:37.510
those out in future episodes of Things You
Missed.

00:13:37.510 --> 00:13:41.680
The movie ends with Gordon passing the test
and Adam failing to achieve his enlightenment,

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:44.620
and as a result, Jigsaw locks him away in
the dark.

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:47.300
“Game over.”

00:13:48.580 --> 00:13:51.760
...but unlike all of the sports leagues right
now, there would be games to look forward

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:55.390
to in the future, and I’m gonna be covering
them right here on CZsWorld.

00:13:55.390 --> 00:13:59.320
Join me next week for my first Saw episode
of Horror History, and the next episode of

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:02.660
Things You Missed where I’ll be covering
my personal favorite film in Saw franchise,

00:14:02.660 --> 00:14:03.660
Saw II.

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:06.690
If you love Saw, you won’t want to miss
out on any of this stuff, so make sure you

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:11.790
subscribe to CZsWorld for new horrors every
week, RING that deathell for notifications

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:14.020
and I’ll see you in the next one.

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:16.450
Assuming we both survive.

